<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>English is hard!</title>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>English is hard!</title>

            <p>
                As we've seen, there are many many ways to write a conditional
                statement.
                For example, <q> if <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m></q> can be written as
                <q>
            <m>P</m> implies <m>Q</m> </q> or
                <q> <m>Q</m> if <m>P</m></q> or
                <q>
            <m>P</m> only if <m>Q</m> </q> and more.
                (See page 211 of your textbook.) This is similar for the
                universal and existential quantifiers.
                For example, instead of <q> for all</q>, we might say
                <q>for any</q>, or <q> for each</q>.
                And instead of <q> there exists</q> we could say
                <q> there is</q>.
                There's also an idea of <q> hidden quantifiers</q>.
                E.g., sometimes instead of starting <q> for all</q> we start
                with <q>if</q> and it has the same meaning.
            </p>

            <p>
                In general, to negate a statement, it's a good idea to write the
                statement using symbols, including any hidden quantifiers.
                On the other hand, sometimes translating a statement into
                different words, can be easier to interpret.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <example>
            <p>
                The statement <m>(\forall x\in\mathbb{R})(x^2\ge 0)</m> can be
                said as
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            For each real number <m>x</m>, <m>x^2\ge 0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The square of every real number is at least <m>0</m>
                            &gt;.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The square of a real number is at least <m>0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>x</m> is a real number, then <m>x^2\ge 0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                The last statement, is a conditional statement, that
                <q> hides</q> the universal quantifier.
            </p>
        </example>

        <example>
            <p>
                The statement <m>(\exists x\in \mathbb{R})(x^2 = 5)</m> can be
                said as
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            There exists a real number <m>x</m> such that
                            <m>x^2=5</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2=5</m> for some real number <m>x</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            There is some real number whose square is <m>5</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2=5</m> has a real number solution.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Here's some statements with hidden quantifiers, hidden
                    conditional statements, or translated into other words.
                    Rewrite the sentences as directed.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If a function <m>y=f(x)</m> is continuous at <m>x=a</m>,
                        then the function is differentiable at <m>x=a</m>.
                        <em>Rewrite with a universal quantifier.</em>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The equation <m>x^3 + x =0 </m> has a solution.
                        <em>Rewrite with an existential quantifier.</em>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Every natural number is a perfect square.
                        <em>Rewrite with a universal and existential quantifier.</em>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Every real number can be squared.
                        <em>Rewrite with a universal and existential quantifier.</em>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        No basketball players also play tennis.
                        <em>Rewrite as a conditional statement.</em>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Any prime number is not followed by a perfect square.
                        <em>Rewrite as a conditional statement.</em>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity workspace=".5in">
            <introduction>
                <p workspace=".2in">
                    Let <m>a</m> be a real number and let <m>f</m> be a
                    real-valued function defined on an interval containing
                    <m>x=a</m>.
                    Consider the following conditional statement:
                </p>

                <p workspace=".2in">
                    If <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>x=a</m>, then <m>f</m>
                    is continuous at <m>x=a</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Let <m>P</m> be <q> <m>f</m> is differentiable </q>, and
                    <m>Q</m> be <q> <m>f</m> is continuous</q>.
                    So the above statement is <m>P\implies Q</m>.
                    For each of the following write in symbols, using <m>P</m>,
                    <m>Q</m>, and logical operators.
                    Then, determine which statements are equivalent to
                    <m>P\implies Q</m> and which are the negation?
                    (Some may be neither.)
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>x=a</m>, then <m>f</m>
                        is differentiable at <m>x=a</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f</m> is not differentiable at <m>x=a</m>, then
                        <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=a</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=a</m>, then
                        <m>f</m> is not differentiable at <m>x=a</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f</m> is not differentiable at <m>x=a</m> or <m>f</m>
                        is continuous at <m>x=a</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=a</m> or <m>f</m> is
                        differentiable at <m>x=a</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>x=a</m> and <m>f</m> is
                        not continuous at <m>x=a</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity xml:id="act-multipleallexist" workspace=".5in">
            <title>Quantifiers, in Multiples</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Determine if each of the following are true or false.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\forall n\in\mathbb{N})(n \text{ is odd})</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\exists n\in\mathbb{N})(n \text{ is odd})</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\forall x\in \mathbb{R})(\forall y\in\mathbb{R})(xy=1)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\exists x\in \mathbb{R})(\exists y\in\mathbb{R})(xy=1)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\forall x\in \mathbb{R})(\exists y\in\mathbb{R})(xy=1)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\exists x\in \mathbb{R})(\forall y\in\mathbb{R})(xy=1)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\forall x\in \mathbb{R})(\exists y\in\mathbb{R})(x+y=0)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\exists x\in \mathbb{R})(\forall y\in\mathbb{R})(x+y=0)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\forall x\in \mathbb{R})(\exists y\in\mathbb{R})(xy=0)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task >
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(\exists x\in \mathbb{R})(\forall y\in\mathbb{R})(xy=0)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity workspace=".6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    (If time:) Negate the statements in
                    <xref ref="act-multipleallexist"/> and translate into
                    English.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>
    </page>
</handout>